00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:37.280
In 2020, LG's new CEO Kwon Bong-seok&nbsp;
wanted to turn this around.&nbsp;

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:40.720
And he made a very bold proclamation.
[Kwon Bong-seok, LG CEO]: "LG&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:46.000
Electronics' mobile business is&nbsp;
going to be profitable by 2021."&nbsp;

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:52.400
And for context, this was the CEO of the&nbsp;
entirety of LG, not just the head of mobile.&nbsp;

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:57.600
Clearly, LG wanted mobile to work.
But the writing was on the wall.&nbsp;

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:04.302
The last time they had any profit was Q2 2015.
So, what was the plan?

00:08:04.302 --> 00:08:08.400
[Kwon Bong-seok]: "I can say we can make&nbsp;
that happen as LG Electronics will expand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:14.800
our mobile lineup and steadily release new ones&nbsp;
attached with some wow factors to woo consumers."&nbsp;

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:20.320
But this right here was actually&nbsp;
Kwon, and LG's, second big mistake.&nbsp;

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:27.760
Not the profitable part, but the "wow factors".
You might be able to tell what LG was going to do.&nbsp;

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:31.360
But before we get to that,&nbsp;
we need a bit more context.&nbsp;

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:34.160
LG's broader strategy was a bit weird.&nbsp;

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:40.880
It was essentially a paradox — extreme&nbsp;
innovation, but also cheap phones?&nbsp;

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:46.720
Priced around cheaper Samsungs and Huawei.
But LG did have a stroke of luck.

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:25.520
With new sanctions against a huge competitor&nbsp;
— particularly one at the cheaper price&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:31.520
point — LG's prices would seem more competitive.
That demand for that price range had to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:38.080
somewhere. So, less Huawei, more room for LG.
So LG got to work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:44.080
with a focus on "wooing" customers.
And what came next really was impressive.&nbsp;

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:49.200
The LG Velvet, for example — a mid-range&nbsp;
smartphone — had a dual-screen case. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:52.880
separate physical second display&nbsp;
you could clip onto the phone.

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:57.280
This was… actually really cool.
It was basically like the large new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.280 --> 00:10:03.760
flip phones we have today, but in 2020.
It was sort of like a Nintendo DS!&nbsp;

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:09.360
And that was just the beginning.
Then came the LG Wing, a phone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:14.640
with a second screen on a mechanical&nbsp;
swivel that rotated out into a T-shape.

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:22.880
Just imagine being a case designer for this thing.
Then, in January 2021, LG revealed the Rollable.

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:27.280
A phone with an extendable&nbsp;
display. Extremely cool!&nbsp;

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:32.720
LG was showing off some of the most&nbsp;
innovative phones people had ever seen.&nbsp;

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:35.600
But here's where the problems start.&nbsp;

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:39.920
First, we see more of the&nbsp;
earlier problems: commitment.&nbsp;

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:45.920
Even eight months after launching the Wing, people&nbsp;
were still waiting on an update to Android 11.&nbsp;

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:54.080
By this point it was still on Android 10, despite&nbsp;
a promise from LG to upgrade it to 11 through 13.&nbsp;

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:58.160
They also "silently switched to&nbsp;
bi-monthly security patch updates".&nbsp;

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:02.160
It was hard to buy into these cool new&nbsp;
phones when it felt like LG would drop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:07.280
them and move on to the next cool idea.
"The LG Wing feels like abandonware at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:11.760
this point, with LG affording the&nbsp;
device awful software support."&nbsp;

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:15.840
But LG had another problem.
And we see a symptom of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:22.960
this very clearly in LG's flagship.
Snapdragon 865, 5G, great audio recording,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.960 --> 00:11:28.560
octa-core, 64-megapixel dual camera.
It had a lot of the same specs as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:37.920
Galaxy S20, and was over $100 cheaper.
But its name was the LG V60 ThinQ 5G.

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:43.280
Though to be honest, Samsung isn't the best&nbsp;
at this either. There's the Note, the A,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:47.840
the J, and the M, each supposedly&nbsp;
targeting a different customer.&nbsp;

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:52.160
There's also the Z now, which&nbsp;
I think is the foldable one?&nbsp;

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:57.680
But at least their core lineup is&nbsp;
well named: Galaxy S26 or S26 Plus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.680 --> 00:12:02.320
named after the year.
LG, what are we doing here?&nbsp;

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:07.600
And if you thought Samsung's names were a bit&nbsp;
annoying, just look at the rest of LG's lineup.

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:11.120
I think they might have used&nbsp;
every letter of the alphabet.&nbsp;

00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:15.600
And this was part of the second&nbsp;
big mistake LG kept making.&nbsp;

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:18.080
They weren't good at marketing.

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:22.320
LG and Kwon wanted to "woo"&nbsp;
customers — a nice idea.&nbsp;

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:27.280
And their phones definitely did&nbsp;
that, but only with hardware.&nbsp;

00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:32.320
They couldn't actually sell these things.
You have to remember, LG had basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:37.440
disappeared from the minds of most customers.
They had fallen to roughly 2% global smartphone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:43.600
share and shipped only 23 million phones&nbsp;
that year, versus 256 million for Samsung.&nbsp;

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:49.520
Many of their marketing campaigns and&nbsp;
advertisements were sort of… functional?

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:05.440
They lacked the boldness&nbsp;
that Apple and Samsung had.&nbsp;

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:10.320
This might sound weird, but LG felt like&nbsp;
the engineers who designed the phone were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:17.520
also in charge of naming it and marketing it.
Some of you might go "that's exactly what I want".&nbsp;

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:21.440
But it meant LG struggled to read wider markets.&nbsp;

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:25.600
Even with cool features, they&nbsp;
couldn't grab people's attention.&nbsp;

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:32.400
I think if LG had spent a bit less on innovation&nbsp;
and instead hired a really killer marketing VP,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:38.560
things would have been very different.
They might have at least had some good names.&nbsp;

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:41.840
But that was only two of&nbsp;
the three mistakes LG made.&nbsp;

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:53.600
These crazy new phones might have even&nbsp;
worked, if they hadn't made the third mistake.

00:13:53.600 --> 00:14:00.880
We just passed 900,000 subscribers, and&nbsp;
I just want to say thank you all so much.&nbsp;

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:06.240
This ride has been awesome, and&nbsp;
we're almost at 1 million now!

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:11.040
Many phone companies — and tech in general&nbsp;
— typically have two types of products.&nbsp;

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:17.680
The first are the ones LG loved to make: the&nbsp;
crazy ideas, things people had never seen before.&nbsp;

00:14:17.680 --> 00:14:24.080
And the second, which LG lacked: the cash cow.
Your mass-market, solid phone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:30.640
which importantly had good margins.
You might say "okay… phones should make money."&nbsp;

00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:34.320
But these two actually have&nbsp;
an important relationship.&nbsp;

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:40.560
Even if innovative phones lose millions,&nbsp;
that's okay — because the cash cow funds them.&nbsp;

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:46.240
For example, the Galaxy S24 sold 37&nbsp;
million units in 2024, and the Galaxy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.240 --> 00:14:53.440
Z — the foldable — sold just 5.2 million.
They likely made a loss due to big R&amp;D costs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:57.280
but that's okay, because the cash cow&nbsp;
makes enough profit to cover those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.280 --> 00:15:03.280
losses, so eventually, the more&nbsp;
innovative one will make profit.

00:15:03.280 --> 00:15:09.600
These new ideas get iterated on, refined,&nbsp;
and some make it to the mass market.&nbsp;

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:16.000
LG didn't really have this dynamic.
The LG V60 ThinQ 5G sold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:21.200
just 300,000 units globally.
In reality, it was the rest of LG keeping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.200 --> 00:15:27.760
the entire mobile division alive. Not good.
By this point, it was too late.

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:33.440
In January 2021, Kwon was sending internal&nbsp;
messages to staff suggesting LG was looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:38.560
at closing their mobile business.
For context, less than a month earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:42.800
LG was showing off the Rollable.
LG's smartphones had now recorded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.800 --> 00:15:48.960
losses for 23 consecutive quarters,&nbsp;
the last profitable one being Q2 2015.&nbsp;

00:15:48.960 --> 00:15:56.560
The total loss was $4.5 billion across six years.
The division was also only bringing in 7% of total&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.560 --> 00:16:01.840
revenue, so it was dragging everything&nbsp;
else down without contributing much.&nbsp;

00:16:01.840 --> 00:16:08.747
So on April 5th, 2021, LG announced that&nbsp;
it was closing its mobile business unit.&nbsp;

00:16:08.747 --> 00:16:10.000
[Ko Eui-young, analyst, Hi Investment&nbsp;
&amp; Securities]: "In the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:15.360
LG targeted mid-priced — if not ultra-low&nbsp;
— models, and that means Samsung, which has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.360 --> 00:16:20.160
more mid-priced product lines than Apple,&nbsp;
will be better able to attract LG users."&nbsp;

00:16:20.160 --> 00:16:23.920
But after the announcement,&nbsp;
something interesting happened.&nbsp;

00:16:23.920 --> 00:16:26.625
Their share price surged 12 percent.
[Koh Jeong-woo, analyst at NH Investment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.625 --> 00:16:31.040
&amp; Securities]: "Investors now have a clearer&nbsp;
sense of the fate of the smartphone business.&nbsp;

00:16:31.040 --> 00:16:35.680
We are not living in a time where every&nbsp;
smartphone maker grows and makes money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.680 --> 00:16:41.120
LG failed to turn it around with the release&nbsp;
of its highly anticipated LG Wing and couldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:47.120
benefit from U.S. sanctions against Huawei."
Even with those big sanctions and restrictions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.120 --> 00:16:51.280
LG phones were already dying,&nbsp;
had very little consumer pull,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.280 --> 00:16:56.640
and people likely went to Xiaomi and Oppo&nbsp;
instead, or the cheaper Samsung brands.&nbsp;

00:16:56.640 --> 00:17:01.600
I will note, though, a lot of&nbsp;
consumers were pretty sad about this.&nbsp;

00:17:01.600 --> 00:17:06.880
"LG were never perfect but in a world of&nbsp;
boring slabs they delivered some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.880 --> 00:17:12.320
most unique phone designs, ideas and&nbsp;
features ever. Time to pour one out."&nbsp;

00:17:12.320 --> 00:17:19.440
"LG has some of the best audio hardware of any&nbsp;
phone. It's a shame that LG is withdrawing."

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:24.000
And I feel the same way.
The once third-biggest, and one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:31.920
important phone brands, was dead. This probably&nbsp;
meant more of the same, and less innovation.&nbsp;

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:38.480
But, as much as I wish LG phones were still around&nbsp;
today, they made the right business decision.

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:44.640
LG phones were dragging the entire company&nbsp;
down, so it was better to cut losses.&nbsp;

00:17:44.640 --> 00:17:48.800
The worst case would have been pouring&nbsp;
billions more into R&amp;D because of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.800 --> 00:17:54.720
sunk cost fallacy, making things even worse,&nbsp;
and potentially ruining the entire company.&nbsp;

00:17:54.720 --> 00:18:01.200
Interestingly, Kwon Bong-seok was actually&nbsp;
promoted to Vice-Chairman some time after this.

00:18:01.200 --> 00:18:06.080
But what makes all of this even crazier is&nbsp;
that while their phone division was dying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:11.920
LG was making an even bigger gamble&nbsp;
— in TVs, specifically with OLED.&nbsp;

00:18:11.920 --> 00:18:15.360
They were about 10 times as&nbsp;
